---
/**
 * The findings page. Conclusions first, each linking to the study that established it.
 *
 * Every number is computed from `eval/results/*.json` at build time — see `src/lib/research.ts`.
 * A page that argues for measuring prose cannot quote a figure it typed by hand.
 */
import { getCollection } from "astro:content"

import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro"
import { corpusTotals, run, variantRows } from "../../lib/research"

const studies = (await getCollection("research")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order)
const totals = corpusTotals()

const byId = (id: string) => studies.find((s) => s.data.slug === id)

// The three headline effects, from the within-run control in run 3.
const r3 = run("3")!
const r3rows = variantRows(r3)
const ctrl = r3rows.find((v) => v.variant === "A")!
const rules = r3rows.find((v) => v.variant === "E")!

// The label result, from run 5.
const r5 = variantRows(run("5")!)
const r5ctrl = r5.find((v) => v.variant === "A")!
const r5e = r5.find((v) => v.variant === "E")!
const r5l = r5.find((v) => v.variant === "L")!

const cut = (arm: { words: number }, control: { words: number }) =>
  Math.round(100 * (1 - arm.words / control.words))

const pct1 = (n: number) => n.toFixed(1)
---

<Base
  title="Research — what actually changes how a model writes"
  description={`Measured findings from ${totals.answers} scored answers across ${totals.runs} runs: what instruction wording moves, what it does not, and what is closed.`}
>
  <div class="band">
    <h1>Research</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      prosemeter was built to answer one question — does a writing instruction actually change how a model writes? —
      and then used to answer it. {totals.answers} generated answers across {totals.runs} runs, every score committed,
      every answer kept.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are the conclusions. Each links to the study that established it. Where a study found the opposite of what
      it set out to show, it says so; three of the six did.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="band tint">
    <h2>What instruction wording moves</h2>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Concrete rewrite rules cut length by about a third</h3>
      <p>
        Measured within a single run against a no-instruction control on the same tasks: {Math.round(ctrl.words)} words
        down to {Math.round(rules.words)}, a {cut(rules, ctrl)}% cut. Jargon fell {
          pct1(ctrl.jargonPct - rules.jargonPct)
        } points and sentence-simplicity rose {pct1(rules.sentenceSimplicity - ctrl.sentenceSimplicity)} points.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("control-arm")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("control-arm")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Naming a technique beats spelling it out</h3>
      <p>
        Replacing one rule with its label — <code>Use BLUF: bottom line up front</code> — and holding the other four
        fixed cut {cut(r5l, r5ctrl)}% off the control against the spelled-out sentence's {cut(r5e, r5ctrl)}%, while tying
        it on jargon, sentence-simplicity, grade band and clarity.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("bluf-as-a-label")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("bluf-as-a-label")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Naming the better word works; asking for fewer does not</h3>
      <p>
        “Avoid jargon” does not reduce jargon. Naming the swap — use over utilize, start over initiate — does. The vague
        instruction lands most of the length win and almost none of the vocabulary win.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("instruction-register")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("instruction-register")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="band">
    <h2>What it does not move</h2>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Factual accuracy, at all</h3>
      <p>
        Two runs and one targeted mechanism test found the same thing: wording moves length and vocabulary two to three
        times over, and moves correctness not at all. The two highest-scoring instructions tied the control for worst
        accuracy. Task difficulty dominated — accuracy spanned 3/15 to 14/15 by task against 15/30 to 21/30 by
        instruction.
      </p>
      <p><strong>A high score is not a correctness signal. Verify facts separately.</strong></p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("qualifier-carveout")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("qualifier-carveout")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Whether an explanation is legible</h3>
      <p>
        A reply scoring 85 drew “I have no idea what you're saying”; the rewrite that landed scored 84. An arm built to
        fix that failure moved the composite by half a point. The instrument cannot see the thing, so it cannot score
        the fix — it can only price it.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("bluf-as-a-label")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("bluf-as-a-label")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="band tint">
    <h2>What the method taught us</h2>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Read the dimensions, not the composite</h3>
      <p>
        The composite is a weighted average, so it averages the real effects away. Run 1 spanned 79.5–86.6 across seven
        instructions while the spread <em>inside</em> a single instruction was about 15 points — so no composite gap in
        it meant anything. The dimensions moved two to three times over the same answers.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("instruction-register")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("instruction-register")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>A metric that works can be an artifact of your task set</h3>
      <p>
        Word count was the sharpest instruction dial on six tasks that were all the same shape, and stopped working on
        ten mixed ones — natural length varies far more when the registers differ. Sentence-simplicity held across both
        and is the one to lean on.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("task-heterogeneity")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("task-heterogeneity")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>Findings are model-specific, and the ranking travels further than the size</h3>
      <p>
        Re-run on Sonnet 5, the winning instruction still won, but moved length 12% where it moved Opus 41% — its
        uninstructed output was already a third shorter, so there was less to win.
      </p>
      <a href={`/research/${byId("multi-model")!.data.slug}`}>{byId("multi-model")!.data.title} →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="result">
      <h3>A dimension that never fires is not thereby useless</h3>
      <p>
        Grade band scores a perfect 100 on 87 of every 100 answers in this corpus, which looked like dead weight. It is
        not: the corpus is answers written by a model asked to be clear, so it contains almost nothing for a ceiling to
        catch. Removing the ceiling let a deliberately-jargon-heavy fixture score 100 at reading grade 25.8.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="band">
    <h2>The studies</h2>
    <ul class="studies">
      {
        studies.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/research/${s.data.slug}`}>{s.data.title}</a>
            <p class="finding">{s.data.finding}</p>
            <p class="meta">
              run {s.data.run} · {s.data.date}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2>The data</h2>
    <p>
      Every answer and every score is in the repository. {totals.answers} answers across {totals.runs} runs, on {
        totals.models.join(" and ")
      }, with {totals.reviewed} carrying a reviewer's finding of a specific technical error.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Those errors are the point, not a defect.</strong> The finding that wording does not reach accuracy can
      only be shown by keeping the wrong answers beside the right ones, with the instruction that produced each. Every
      file says in its own front matter that it is experiment output and was never fact-checked as documentation.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/jordanburke/prosemeter/tree/main/eval/corpus">eval/corpus/</a> — the answers,
        marked
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/jordanburke/prosemeter/tree/main/eval/results">eval/results/</a> — every dimension
        score, stamped with the engine version that produced it
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/jordanburke/prosemeter/blob/main/eval/README.md">eval/README.md</a> — the method,
        and the list of things that do not need re-deriving
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .standfirst {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  /* margin-block, never the `margin` shorthand: `.band > *` supplies `margin-inline: auto`, and a
     shorthand with a `0` in the inline slot silently resets it and pins the box to the left edge.
     Cost an afternoon once already on the scorer panel. */
  .result {
    margin-block: 2rem;
  }
  .result h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .result > a {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  ul.studies {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  ul.studies li {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  ul.studies .finding {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.2rem;
  }
  ul.studies .meta {
    margin: 0;
    font: 0.85rem var(--mono);
    color: var(--dim);
  }
</style>
